{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2253 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "0bfaab8457f801caf1a1d240ac758fbca4a0b4a7795a8e38d83a50ef2abaeb83" :edb/db-before-t 2252 :edb/db-after-t 2253 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535565 50 #inst "2026-10-09T02:37:18.513Z" 13194139535565 true] [17592186092127 1020 #edb/ref 17592186355397 13194139535565 true] [17592186092128 1020 #edb/ref 17592186355380 13194139535565 true] [17592186092128 1020 #edb/ref 17592186355387 13194139535565 true] [17592186092228 1020 #edb/ref 17592186355390 13194139535565 true] [17592186092682 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535565 true] [17592186092682 1100 "For which of the following graphs would the trapezoidal rule give an overestimate of the area under the curve between $x =-1$ and $x = 3$?" 13194139535565 false] [17592186092682 1100 "For which of the following graphs would the trapezoidal rule give an overestimate of the area under the curve between $x=-1$ and $x=3?$" 13194139535565 true] [17592186092682 1145 "The trapezoidal rule overestimates the area under the curve if the function is concave up over the given interval.    \n\nFrom the given graphs, the only curve that is concave up on $[-1,3]$ is the one shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067661/assets/e5663868b6caa013097b70233d9461227410f9dd6be9113620a67501b8466abf.png)" 13194139535565 true] [17592186355380 1017 #edb/ref 17592186355381 13194139535565 true] [17592186355380 1089 "q-116621" 13194139535565 true] [17592186355380 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535565 true] [17592186355380 1100 "The following table shows the number of customers per minute, $c(t),$ that arrive at a shop counter over a period of $120$ minutes.\n\n| $t\\,(\\text{minutes})$ | $0$ | $30$ | $80$ | $120$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $c(t)\\,(\\text{customers/minute})$ | $10$ | $8$ | $6$ | $12$ |\n\nUse the trapezoidal rule with the partition\n\n$$\n[0,30]\\cup [30,80]\\cup [80,120],\n$$\n\nto approximate the total number of customers served in two hours." 13194139535565 true] [17592186355380 1113 #uuid "28a6dc88-cc83-5e2f-ad4d-41299804d15f" 13194139535565 true] [17592186355380 1145 "In this context, we have $3$ trapezoids, and the area $A$ under the curve $y=c(t)$ represents the total number of customers served in $2$ hours.\n\nUsing the trapezoidal rule, we estimate the area as follows:\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{c(0)+c(30)}{2}\\cdot Δ{t}_{1}+\\frac{c(30)+c(80)}{2}\\cdot Δ{t}_{2}+\\frac{c(80)+c(120)}{2}\\cdot Δ{t}_{3}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the appropriate numbers into the above expression gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈\\frac{10+8}{2}\\cdot (30-0)+\\frac{8+6}{2}\\cdot (80-30)+\\frac{6+12}{2}\\cdot (120-80) \\\\  & =9\\cdot 30+7\\cdot 50+9\\cdot 40 \\\\  & =980\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total number of customers serve approximately is $980$ in $2$ hours." 13194139535565 true] [17592186355381 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355382 13194139535565 true] [17592186355381 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355383 13194139535565 true] [17592186355381 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355384 13194139535565 true] [17592186355381 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355385 13194139535565 true] [17592186355381 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355386 13194139535565 true] [17592186355381 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535565 true] [17592186355381 1041 "selection" 13194139535565 true] [17592186355381 1104 #edb/ref 17592186355385 13194139535565 true] [17592186355381 1110 #uuid "6d211b4e-ff2a-53ac-9910-c22000b388af" 13194139535565 true] [17592186355382 1034 "1\\,020" 13194139535565 true] [17592186355382 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535565 true] [17592186355382 1111 #uuid "5b7af875-8f86-5068-981f-28022f2d00fd" 13194139535565 true] [17592186355383 1034 "770" 13194139535565 true] [17592186355383 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535565 true] [17592186355383 1111 #uuid "7af771c3-4e6c-526b-aec4-2b21a13cea87" 13194139535565 true] [17592186355384 1034 "890" 13194139535565 true] [17592186355384 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535565 true] [17592186355384 1111 #uuid "12561444-5c40-5e0e-a78a-6be8584ceecd" 13194139535565 true] [17592186355385 1034 "980" 13194139535565 true] [17592186355385 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535565 true] [17592186355385 1111 #uuid "eb866571-56be-54d8-a406-9209672bc5b7" 13194139535565 true] [17592186355386 1034 "1\\,000" 13194139535565 true] [17592186355386 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535565 true] [17592186355386 1111 #uuid "1c4dc78f-49c1-5da3-b01c-9aa3b6e02ce9" 13194139535565 true] [17592186355387 1017 #edb/ref 17592186355388 13194139535565 true] [17592186355387 1089 "q-315743" 13194139535565 true] [17592186355387 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535565 true] [17592186355387 1100 "The following table shows the inflow rate of water, $q(t),$ in $\\text{L/min},$ into a storage tank over a period of $90$ minutes.\n\n| $t\\,(\\text{minutes})$ | $0$ | $20$ | $60$ | $90$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $q(t)\\,(\\text{L/min})$ | $15$ | $25$ | $20$ | $10$ |\n\nUse the trapezoidal rule with the partition\n\n$$\n[0,20]\\cup [20,60]\\cup [60,90],\n$$\n\nto approximate the total volume of water that flowed into the tank in $90$ minutes.\n\nThe total volume is {{field-1}} liters." 13194139535565 true] [17592186355387 1113 #uuid "5ea5af74-d9e6-5ec0-a5a9-05bf839b14a5" 13194139535565 true] [17592186355387 1145 "In this context, we have $3$ trapezoids, and the area $A$ under the curve $y=q(t)$ represents the total volume of water that flowed into the tank in $90$ minutes.\n\nUsing the trapezoidal rule, we estimate the area as follows:\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{q(0)+q(20)}{2}\\cdot Δ{t}_{1}+\\frac{q(20)+q(60)}{2}\\cdot Δ{t}_{2}+\\frac{q(60)+q(90)}{2}\\cdot Δ{t}_{3}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the appropriate numbers into the above expression gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈\\frac{15+25}{2}\\cdot (20-0)+\\frac{25+20}{2}\\cdot (60-20)+\\frac{20+10}{2}\\cdot (90-60) \\\\  & =20\\cdot 20+22.5\\cdot 40+15\\cdot 30 \\\\  & =400+900+450 \\\\  & =1750.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total volume of water that flowed into the tank is $1750$ liters." 13194139535565 true] [17592186355388 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355389 13194139535565 true] [17592186355388 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535565 true] [17592186355388 1041 "field-1" 13194139535565 true] [17592186355388 1104 #edb/ref 17592186355389 13194139535565 true] [17592186355388 1110 #uuid "8c78c5ae-39ac-51c4-8f3c-ea9c526a3b28" 13194139535565 true] [17592186355389 1034 "1750" 13194139535565 true] [17592186355389 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535565 true] [17592186355389 1111 #uuid "ea913ff8-58a5-5fda-9f60-639c6f082d73" 13194139535565 true] [17592186355390 1017 #edb/ref 17592186355391 13194139535565 true] [17592186355390 1089 "q-85673" 13194139535565 true] [17592186355390 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535565 true] [17592186355390 1100 "For which of the following graphs would the trapezoidal rule give an underestimate of the area under the curve between $x=-1$ and $x=1?$" 13194139535565 true] [17592186355390 1113 #uuid "0f96be30-b6f7-514c-9651-7513613567d5" 13194139535565 true] [17592186355390 1145 "The trapezoidal rule underestimates the area under the curve if the function is concave down over the given interval.     \n\nFrom the given graphs, the only curve that is concave down on $[-1,1]$ is the one shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067661/assets/a8bcad7d4e90c6fdde99189026764d2b07a0709b8450252250c4fa133f5ceec0.png)" 13194139535565 true] [17592186355391 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355392 13194139535565 true] [17592186355391 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355393 13194139535565 true] [17592186355391 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355394 13194139535565 true] [17592186355391 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355395 13194139535565 true] [17592186355391 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355396 13194139535565 true] [17592186355391 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535565 true] [17592186355391 1041 "selection" 13194139535565 true] [17592186355391 1104 #edb