"q-85683" 13194139535565 true] [17592186355397 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535565 true] [17592186355397 1100 "Using the trapezoidal rule with the partition\n\n$$\n[-1,2]=[-1,0]\\cup [0,2],\n$$\n\napproximate the area under the curve of the function $y=\\frac{10}{1+{x}^{2}}.$" 13194139535565 true] [17592186355397 1113 #uuid "9177065a-e07c-56ec-9cd6-608f8a33a326" 13194139535565 true] [17592186355397 1145 "Let $f(x)=\\frac{10}{1+{x}^{2}}.$ Let's plot this function:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067661/assets/c4c9aa8e615747199462e0597f4cb7be82031efdb5b2f6585e3fb9c0b652ee78.png)\n\nWe've drawn some trapezoids to help calculate our trapezoidal rule sum. By calculating the area of each trapezoid, we obtain the following approximation for our area $A:$\n\n$$\nA≈\\overset{1\\text{st trapezoid}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\frac{\\overset{\\text{bases}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(-1)+f(0)}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,{{Δ{x}_{1}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}}+\\overset{2\\text{nd trapezoid}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\frac{\\overset{\\text{bases}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0)+f(2)}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,{{Δ{x}_{2}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the appropriate numbers into the above expression gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈\\frac{5+10}{2}\\cdot (0-(-1))+\\frac{10+2}{2}\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}\\cdot 1+\\frac{12}{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}+12 \\\\  & =\\frac{39}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535565 true] [17592186355398 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355399 13194139535565 true] [17592186355398 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355400 13194139535565 true] [17592186355398 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355401 13194139535565 true] [17592186355398 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355402 13194139535565 true] [17592186355398 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355403 13194139535565 true] [17592186355398 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535565 true] [17592186355398 1041 "selection" 13194139535565 true] [17592186355398 1104 #edb/ref 17592186355401 13194139535565 true] [17592186355398 1110 #uu